{"name":"Agave Plant","hex":"#879c67","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"agave","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant in the large, variable genus Agave of succulent plants, commonly armed with formidable prickles, flowering at maturity after several years, and generally dying thereafter; large species, such as the maguey or century plant, (Agave americana), produce gigantic inflorescences. Several are of economic importance as sources of fibre such as sisal, and alcoholic beverages such as tequila."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/agave"},{"term":"plant","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An organism that is not an animal, especially an organism capable of photosynthesis, and typically a small or herbaceous organism of this kind, rather than a tree.","An organism of the kingdom Plantae, and now specifically, a living organism of the Embryophyta (land plants) or of the Chlorophyta (green algae), a eukaryote that includes double-membraned chloroplasts in its cells containing chlorophyll a and b, or any organism closely related to such an organism."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To place (a seed or plant) in soil or other substrate in order that it may live and grow.","To furnish or supply with plants."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plant"}],"entries":[{"title":"Agave","blocks":[{"text":"Agave is a genus of monocots native to the arid regions of the Americas. The genus is primarily known for its succulent and xerophytic species that typically form large rosettes of strong, fleshy leaves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Agave_americana_R01.jpg/330px-Agave_americana_R01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agave"},{"title":"Plant","blocks":[{"text":"Plants are the eukaryotic organisms that constitute the kingdom Plantae. They are predominantly photosynthetic or came from photosynthetic ancestors, meaning that they obtain their energy from sunlight by using the green pigment chlorophyll in their chloroplasts to produce sugars from carbon dioxide and water. Over the course of time, some plants have evolved to be parasitic, meaning they take energy from other organisms. Except for glaucophytes, some green algae and a few red algae, most plants are multicellular."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Fr%C3%BChling_bl%C3%BChender_Kirschenbaum.jpg/330px-Fr%C3%BChling_bl%C3%BChender_Kirschenbaum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plant"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg/250px-Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg/250px-Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The large flower spike of Agave chiapensis, San Francisco Botanical Garden","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAgave_chiapensis_whole.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Agave_shawii_shawii_rosette_life_cycle.png/500px-Agave_shawii_shawii_rosette_life_cycle.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Agave_shawii_shawii_rosette_life_cycle.png/1280px-Agave_shawii_shawii_rosette_life_cycle.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Agave_shawii_shawii_rosette_life_cycle.png/500px-Agave_shawii_shawii_roset